#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1200.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180506} :activity/id #uuid "b8ced6b0-8e1a-57eb-9f2f-b4bd8eace852" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050793 :topic/id #uuid "6768fffe-a644-5bd7-b35c-6b2cc2563786" :topic/math-academy-id 3716} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180504 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179744 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179606 :question/id #uuid "c1924df7-205f-512e-b024-72f5a654f062" :question/math-academy-id "e-9560"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c9de11e-2195-5946-a19e-235667d39631" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050666}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548269} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552504}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Parametric Form"} :step/id #uuid "4fded59b-3b34-5c3a-b5bc-fbdf60f1028f" :step/math-academy-id 35826 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180505}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180505 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179745 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179607 :question/id #uuid "e9d8e6c2-d346-52fb-918a-5d5c95cd986c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9561"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ea50c3f-d401-54c5-868c-b67992bf18ee" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048540}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421760}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Cartesian Form"} :step/id #uuid "a52a1b21-2c56-5112-aa12-6e43086599bc" :step/math-academy-id 35827 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180509}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180506 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180725 :tutorial/content "Let $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ be a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ and $C$ be a *closed* curve in the $xy$-plane. Recall that the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $C$ is given by the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nCalculating flux using this definition is often cumbersome because we need to calculate $\\mathbf n,$ the outward-pointing unit normal vector. \n\nIn practice, we often use the following alternative formula to calculate the flux:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_C P\\,\\textrm{d}y- Q\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWe'll derive this formula at the end of the lesson.\n\n**Watch Out!** When applying this formula, we must choose a positively-oriented parameterization for $C.$ This formula will *not* work if $C$ is negatively oriented!" :tutorial/id #uuid "c74e835b-f210-5a77-8d6e-aef474c89522" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5851 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cb889341-22d5-52e6-9979-1fd814bc5cec" :step/math-academy-id 35829 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180504}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180507 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179746 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179608 :question/id #uuid "337d9d7e-5488-5e6d-af34-e01d22d3d0b9" :question/math-academy-id "e-9574"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83a9ea5a-dcc8-51ed-90bc-dd684ef71ca9" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557574} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180039} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421764}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Non-Closed Curve"} :step/id #uuid "f9195b98-bf60-5d80-a9f1-4dbe4c7ec614" :step/math-academy-id 35863 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180510}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180509 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180727 :tutorial/content "As we've seen, the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf F$ through a piecewise-smooth, closed curve $C$ is a unique scalar number that represents the net flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C.$ \n\nThe question now is, does the notion of flux exist for curves that are not closed?\n\nIndeed, it is possible to define the notion of flux through a non-closed curve. However, there is one crucial difference we need to bear in mind:\n\n- In the case of a smooth, closed curve, it is always clear which direction is \"out.\" Therefore, the outward-pointing unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ is unique at every point on $C.$\n\n- However, on a smooth, non-closed curve, there is no clear notion of \"in\" and \"out.\" Instead, we can choose between *two* normal vectors at any point on the curve, as shown below.\n\n![](images/d1/d193eb8109ead15de50c06a7bb0b654243173d882bd361f9fd34026aa23c1b5f.png)\n\nTherefore, when computing the flux of a vector field through a non-closed curve, we first specify the direction that the unit normal points and then measure the flux relative to that direction. \n\nFor example, for the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and curve $C$ below, one choice for the unit normal will give a positive number, and the other a negative number.\n\n![](images/4f/4f92dd5334b5836703857c081049f126d39c141e84d8e94171e338a162cf3cc4.png)\n\nFinally, to calculate the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across a non-closed curve $C,$ we can continue to use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!** To apply this formula, we usually need to parameterize $C.$ It is *vital* that we pick a parameterization where the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ is a $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* rotation of the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e2794e26-620d-59e9-85de-a3bb49f6991a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5869 :tutorial/title "Calculating Flux for Non-Closed Curves"} :step/id #uuid "c6cc7967-4281-527a-8021-7768a792728f" :step/math-academy-id 35901 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180507}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180510 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180728 :tutorial/content "Let's now derive the following formula for the flux:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_C P\\,\\textrm{d}y- Q\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that if $C$ is parameterized by the function $\\mathbf r(t) = x(t)\\,\\mathbf i + y(t)\\,\\mathbf j$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ then \n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\dfrac{y'(t)\\,\\mathbf i - x'(t)\\,\\mathbf j}{||\\mathbf r'(t)||},\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that $C$ is positively-oriented. \n\nSubstituting our expression for $\\mathbf n$ into our line integral definition of flux and using the fact that $\\textrm d s = ||\\mathbf r'(t)|| \\,\\textrm d t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(P(x(t),y(t))\\,i+Q(x(t),y(t))\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)\\,i-{x}^{′}(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||})||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}(P(x(t),y(t))\\,i+Q(x(t),y(t))\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)\\,i-{x}^{′}(t)\\,j}{\\cancel{||{r}^{′}(t)||}})\\cancel{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}(P(x(t),y(t))\\,i+Q(x(t),y(t))\\,j)\\cdot ({y}^{′}(t)\\,i-{x}^{′}(t)\\,j)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P(x(t),y(t)){y}^{′}(t)-Q(x(t),y(t)){x}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cc71a06d-4832-5949-91b4-71cb0f8185ca" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5870 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Main Formula"} :step/id #uuid "7543ee2e-ca36-5209-8a6f-1fae02154a3d" :step/math-academy-id 35902}] :activity/title "Calculating Flux in Two-Dimensional Vector Fields" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179867}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547122} :activity/id #uuid "29222382-7ee7-520a-a03b-87b48ac36cf4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050793 :topic/id #uuid "6768fffe-a644-5bd7-b35c-6b2cc2563786" :topic/math-academy-id 3716} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547097 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262721 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d6e8dc0-de0b-5246-baf1-2e88a94ead89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260835} {:answer/id #uuid "86aa0ca4-2fef-5ea7-ab52-7326ec1150e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-75\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261048} {:answer/id #uuid "945c2bc9-3eea-5671-ac58-7ea3e0a8ed17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "150\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261168} {:answer/id #uuid "b7033798-e10d-5128-ab9b-7bc561366c39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-100\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261481} {:answer/id #uuid "dcaaf559-e7cf-5424-90bb-d31bc261f4d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "50\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261824}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260835} :answer-field/id #uuid "23816b2f-7629-5e86-9bf6-14c8e6ebed0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5660b13f-68bb-56f7-8d7c-83ccf48f3c64" :question/math-academy-id "q-127149" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i-5\\,j$ across the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={5}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is a circle centered at the origin with the radius $5.$ It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the double-angle formula for cosine, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-5)\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }25{cos}^{2}⁡t-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =25{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1}{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =25(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =25(0+\\pi +1)-(0+1) \\\\  & =25\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "244028d0-80f0-53fe-985b-068cd754088c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547151}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547122 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262715 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e1109ed-b141-519b-a9c0-7e14a71dad93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260290} {:answer/id #uuid "9c868b9a-1bd9-5569-9736-5eeff1693bf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261230} {:answer/id #uuid "d2368728-193a-54c0-a8d0-19ec801f6e17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261726} {:answer/id #uuid "d923b6e6-b8d4-5b4d-b3b3-1e5f1ccd3861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261792} {:answer/id #uuid "ff0167ac-6b4e-5d5c-aee1-3a42390d42e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262111}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261230} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e7bee21-7d21-5f74-b594-30d563e58eac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "22f1827e-9056-5ca5-89c4-8b8f59040ce8" :question/math-academy-id "q-127142" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=7\\,i+2\\,j$ across the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nThe closed curve $C$ is a circle centered at the origin with the radius $6.$ It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =7, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(7)\\cdot (6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(2)\\cdot (-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }42\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(42\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(0-12)-(0-12) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8a5caabe-eb94-58e0-9030-d7df067be15b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547176}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547123 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262738 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b4f1bb5-5af8-54dc-b770-fd3f05a8066c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260522} {:answer/id #uuid "5d592e34-b162-5fde-8afc-943efe3e8ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260678} {:answer/id #uuid "ad49cef3-b98d-5033-a436-5ffab9049391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261392} {:answer/id #uuid "aeb8232e-688e-5fce-98f8-d1fc7d49d436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261415} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb10982-6a16-5384-8d48-742898dfa1f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262100}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260522} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c9c8acf-0dab-51c5-ade8-bb82d29c0b7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262202}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d7df129a-7685-5a03-8c7b-340092011e9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-127233" :question/problem "![](images/9b/9bb15934c271a17851342b9032f5096f48e7ac9ee4e149af92ac1010dea46d88.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=3xy\\,i+(2x+1)\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)=t\\,i+t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nIn this case, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula. \n\n![](images/0e/0e751adb9f44d24ba89af318fe5f063b1be146bbae45d05fc2dd9a99bef259f2.png)\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =3xy=3{t}^{2}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =2x+1=2t+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{t}^{2})(1)-(2t+1)(1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}-2t-1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{3}-{t}^{2}-t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(1-1-1)-(0-0-0) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8ba5ec6f-d785-5185-aba1-3e57718869aa"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547151 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262734 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fd158a1-8e1c-5409-9f7d-cc713afefd91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260308} {:answer/id #uuid "49e0677d-703c-5fd2-bba6-5d10e8428357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260512} {:answer/id #uuid "afbd580d-7551-55b1-a2ab-a54c76c17535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261427} {:answer/id #uuid "d49ca082-e6c8-5be2-9a1d-8f3250a52777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261749} {:answer/id #uuid "f489697f-1e0a-5833-b73d-49cd2c52c047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262011}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260512} :answer-field/id #uuid "fec87113-a1f7-5b61-8ed5-83eb792efa1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d9d8ed81-5fd6-581e-8bd7-3dd3517c5bfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-127223" :question/problem "![](images/41/414611a90c2227ff163d40a4f6f560bc67c471fa0f35724e459cec280e4904f9.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=4\\,i+2\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\frac{t}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2t}\\,j$ for $t\\in [\\frac{1}{2},2],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nIn this case, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula. \n\n![](images/11/11376e6ef18b151a6dd1a02dd09f20b72bce3160c718f4095b4f08b23721ff1f.png)\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{t}{2},\\,y=\\frac{1}{2t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =4, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2t})=-\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(4)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}})-(2)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}-1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{t}-t){|}_{1/2}^{2} \\\\  & =(1-2)-(4-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ca75fa0e-2e8e-54d5-8bf1-eb9a2e064862" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547123}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547176 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262716 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1393ce9-919a-5a3f-a8c5-3ce471d06ed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261276} {:answer/id #uuid "a40cd9e0-094a-53e2-bea7-72696c9a5f5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261304} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d88754-afb8-5215-960d-6c87c992d632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261315} {:answer/id #uuid "c6e3c02b-04d6-59c7-b162-6bd0c1725f86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261634} {:answer/id #uuid "c73cd479-dfcc-5a7b-abdd-2aab64decf0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261638}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261638} :answer-field/id #uuid "f19e243a-d229-55b5-b933-2f21b1dc4b86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "79c34a7b-b623-515b-8476-bf469364ea95" :question/math-academy-id "q-127143" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={y}^{3}\\,i+{x}^{3}\\,j$ across the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={1}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is a circle centered at the origin with the radius $1.$ It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={y}^{3}={sin}^{3}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & ={x}^{3}={cos}^{3}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({sin}^{3}⁡t)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-({cos}^{3}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t({sin}^{2}+{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fc626686-cd87-5458-bac3-97ad564bc475" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547097}}] :activity/title "Calculating Flux in Two-Dimensional Vector Fields" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546236}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046669} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552541} :activity/id #uuid "ff4e66a1-8671-5481-a241-0ec676642308" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050793 :topic/id #uuid "6768fffe-a644-5bd7-b35c-6b2cc2563786" :topic/math-academy-id 3716} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552541 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180029 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2605ce5a-8cc3-5767-bd42-b4df2fc57fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177846} {:answer/id #uuid "090f3e9e-10f1-5380-9922-651ecfe6ea3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551683} {:answer/id #uuid "6d43715d-8bf7-5ec2-8e40-279a007829e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551922} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c4f06f-dc17-5580-804b-5e960683c34d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552096} {:answer/id #uuid "d72ed648-66b7-5576-93c4-769d0caa8136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552170}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177846} :answer-field/id #uuid "d25cf8ea-2732-5072-883d-71745e726402" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d6daea5f-9d1c-5bf1-b6af-12c3102e71d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-126717" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-x=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1da0af94-7ee7-5f6d-819f-b12e5fd6862d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552564}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552555 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d42fa31-693c-5e63-8b30-f714f163cf61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551774} {:answer/id #uuid "5a97f892-a6ec-5102-a2f8-b11ba307ea5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551880} {:answer/id #uuid "788d4fd5-7983-5bde-ac02-62cb31dfdb02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551948} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd545ee-d36f-5cc5-9368-52d6c0d580da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551960} {:answer/id #uuid "ceda2cc6-35df-59c0-b345-80992bffb35d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552157}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551960} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1fc0cd0-57f3-5df9-9d82-31c607584763" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c696854b-d1cc-50f5-8398-3fba96da4739" :question/math-academy-id "q-127063" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=xi+\\frac{1}{4}\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the double-angle formula for cosine, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(\\frac{1}{4})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }32{cos}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }32(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1}{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+16t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(0+32\\pi -1)-(0-1) \\\\  & =32\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3f3daaae-7b86-5566-920d-102af6de28ae" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552619}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552564 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552492 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52d243fe-78bb-52be-a2f0-f9815804e4d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551859} {:answer/id #uuid "62fe97bc-579e-5ca3-9781-e68777e97322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551899} {:answer/id #uuid "86c104da-5667-58a2-96d7-fb45fe8f70c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551990} {:answer/id #uuid "cc321452-9e74-5980-b3a3-7f6a5738db5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552153} {:answer/id #uuid "f2bc6932-c933-563c-a5c7-9780b5ef00c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552234}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551859} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fe3669d-5fb8-5cf8-8402-eaff7b7d0308" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b2316759-1f4d-59b3-aebb-24c5b1ac8267" :question/math-academy-id "q-127061" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={y}^{2}\\,i-xy\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={y}^{2}=4{sin}^{2}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-xy=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=u,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt=du,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0}6{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6b142e13-547a-5e6b-946c-e441119e22ff" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552555}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552595 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262713 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6893a418-d322-5ab1-83ac-21e6380ffd8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-14\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260788} {:answer/id #uuid "8d1a6f11-e4dc-58f0-b3b9-20f5896018bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261107} {:answer/id #uuid "ad14b8d3-dc2a-51ae-93a4-3065094d2fff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261390} {:answer/id #uuid "c8457952-5981-52f2-8dd0-d726a811dc80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261647} {:answer/id #uuid "e9652b71-0ef3-57bb-b58d-c0c40977ff4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261917}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261917} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4fee625-b03f-59fc-b891-1aff5cd98622" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9148e698-2674-5a60-a839-9ed2c4895150" :question/math-academy-id "q-127139" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={x}^{2}=49{cos}^{2}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=u,du=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(49{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(1)\\cdot (-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }98{cos}^{3}⁡t+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }98{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }98(1-{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }98(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }98\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }-98{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }98\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{0}-98{u}^{2}\\,du \\\\  & =(98\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{0}^{2\\pi }+0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b7045b2d-6d96-5e8c-a2ed-01e6f608b548"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552619 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552430 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d2b2827-fc94-5958-9a7b-16367fc19078" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551698} {:answer/id #uuid "5bdc41c8-37d7-56e6-9cda-59976d63909e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551887} {:answer/id #uuid "6c06907e-68fd-5846-846e-61d3ebbdcfb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551915} {:answer/id #uuid "82ba78bb-4833-5028-9cf9-72e3cb59f32f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551975} {:answer/id #uuid "e9e5bb54-585e-59f5-8426-0a44c3083849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552213}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552213} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2ae6c02-1c78-5b5d-926b-2d33957e08c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4696ec15-27d3-55f9-a25e-60bb43e6e055" :question/math-academy-id "q-127075" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={y}^{3}\\,i+(2+x)\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={y}^{3}=8{sin}^{3}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =2+x=2+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=u,du=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(8{sin}^{3}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(2+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }16{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }16{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{0}16{u}^{3}+16u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }+0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f91e58fd-d6a0-5c91-88ea-1cd8e6321190" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552595}}] :activity/title "Calculating Flux in Two-Dimensional Vector Fields" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551665}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046668} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570044} :activity/id #uuid "bf0626c4-30df-57a6-977b-ab7a7032a894" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050793 :topic/id #uuid "6768fffe-a644-5bd7-b35c-6b2cc2563786" :topic/math-academy-id 3716} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570043 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421762 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "093b5f1d-7084-5cca-af48-56a7c276ea5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421730} {:answer/id #uuid "1755e313-29bb-5235-930c-5480eea8e580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{25e}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421732} {:answer/id #uuid "2a42ddb5-8533-50f3-9230-498c83bae1a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{7e}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421735} {:answer/id #uuid "93070110-72d1-593c-aaa7-f43291b04560" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7e}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421745} {:answer/id #uuid "c7b8c70b-0bf4-5466-85d5-56e8bfa6fc37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25e}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421749}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421730} :answer-field/id #uuid "452bd17e-660a-568a-8108-39d6d0770162" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "90df2f56-7b61-5a49-aa6d-4d0c58b35b14" :question/math-academy-id "q-127062" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={e}^{y}\\,i+{e}^{x}\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={e}^{y}={e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & ={e}^{x}={e}^{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitutions $u=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,du=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt$ and $v=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,dv=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-({e}^{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({e}^{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0}{e}^{u}\\,du-{\\int }_{1}^{1}{e}^{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0639941a-dbef-51ef-941b-1ba2f1461b7a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570078}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570044 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180040 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cfab73e-541e-5dea-a884-264b4bd8ec97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177913} {:answer/id #uuid "1d03d1d4-83e8-5434-ba88-654e772f6034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539535} {:answer/id #uuid "2bedcbb9-3a20-535c-92e0-bda037bb0531" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539560} {:answer/id #uuid "94ef6336-a885-5e0f-9339-fb9e597d51dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539726} {:answer/id #uuid "c61c8d76-f473-524c-b92c-3be06cc40c20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539821}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539535} :answer-field/id #uuid "dab28699-4fc7-5cb2-8f84-87f7666ba43c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4edd3b9a-070a-593a-8f84-1b14657d2c07" :question/math-academy-id "q-127055" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=(x+y)\\,i+(y-x)\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x+y=3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t), \\\\ Q(x,y) & =y-x=3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({cos}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =9(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =18\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "065f4f4c-48e3-590c-b5e4-ef6c92a8335e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570094}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570076 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262714 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8337cc5b-cbe0-5afa-9274-103e3f498697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261015} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8c2924-c012-51ca-aa9f-ebee29edb99a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261114} {:answer/id #uuid "9958d4dc-9c0d-556d-9236-c69dc7ace6dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "192\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261204} {:answer/id #uuid "9c54fcc6-cb8b-5805-9834-9c56673a0e9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261228} {:answer/id #uuid "b08ecf9c-f351-5fc2-9328-9e808bafe62f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "384\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261434}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261434} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2ba8e33-8924-5bcc-b848-378e5d728fa7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fc62686d-15ed-5e7b-a83e-66fbf5f6c46f" :question/math-academy-id "q-127140" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=4x\\,i+4y\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =4x=4(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=32\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =4y=4(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(32\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }192({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }192\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =192t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =192(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =384\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a099945a-938f-5e92-8ad0-e1f19c59133c"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570078 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262711 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b2ab957-0579-5e99-8c2c-f53cec1a64ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260254} {:answer/id #uuid "515d246a-bf62-5921-88e9-813966854ecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260564} {:answer/id #uuid "681d213e-13d8-58eb-9b2e-feeb4d760e83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260785} {:answer/id #uuid "787af263-8fcd-544e-a39d-804db798c96f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260930} {:answer/id #uuid "95210164-0d4e-5860-ac8f-9ab7e9038d97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261171}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260564} :answer-field/id #uuid "b00b1074-3f5c-50fa-8cd5-1bc5332b0ac4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "610929b6-feab-56c5-9108-52a1535e1a43" :question/math-academy-id "q-127081" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=y\\,i+9{x}^{3}\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =9{x}^{3}=9{cos}^{3}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=u,du=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(9{cos}^{3}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9{\\int }_{1}^{1}(u+{u}^{3})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a7dae7eb-2366-50b6-908c-5cdea559afe7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570076}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570094 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262702 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1451c613-e020-5234-9207-ca8e2b426177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260055} {:answer/id #uuid "545e7de3-77c0-5001-a9e8-6b1adc5f1466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{16\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260589} {:answer/id #uuid "78acc197-697f-5085-b977-43333a81c4c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{32\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260932} {:answer/id #uuid "7b79330c-376e-54f4-b1b3-7e784422cfed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260951} {:answer/id #uuid "a87d06f4-05e6-5e25-a200-9da07f1daa38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{32\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261345}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260055} :answer-field/id #uuid "473e948e-e7b1-5fb0-bd43-b3e933074eaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d6160f48-7946-52fa-bf89-f2b84f79ba1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-127060" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=xy\\,i+{x}^{2}\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =xy=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & ={x}^{2}=4{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(4{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{1}-16{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e6c63510-5bd6-5eb2-af63-b34178b9e8e5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570043}}] :activity/title "Calculating Flux in Two-Dimensional Vector Fields" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569440}]}
